.TH CBMAP_REDECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_redeclare \- re-establish the companion type variables for an ordered map handle from another scope
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_redeclare(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares the companion type variables that carry key_t and val_t for an
ordered map handle that already exists. The macro declares no handle,
creates no map and destroys none; it gives back only the type information
that the type-inferred macros need.

Every type-inferred macro gets the key type and the value type of a map from
two ordinary local variables that carry the name of the handle, and that
cbmap_construct(3) and cbmap_declare(3) create beside the handle. Because
they are local declarations, they do not travel with the handle to another
function: inside that function, cbmap_insert(hm_name, ...) and its siblings
name variables that nothing declares there, and the translation unit does
not compile. This macro declares those variables again, so every caller
that passes a cbmap across a function boundary needs it.

The types here must be the types that the caller created the map with,
because the macros use them to read a key, to size a value, and to decide
whether a value is a char * that the map stores by contents.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name of the map handle that already exists
key_t    Key type that the caller created the map with
val_t    Value type that the caller created the map with
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro expands to a sequence of declarations, so put it where a
declaration is legal and give it a semicolon of its own at the end
The declared variables are pointers that nothing dereferences at run time.
They exist so that typeof and the type-introspection macros can read the
types from them, and they cost nothing in the generated code
The macro declares variables only, which also makes it the way to reach a
handle that a struct field or a global holds: name the macro in each
function that works on that handle
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The declared value type can have a width that differs from the stored
width. cbmap_get(3) and cbmap_get_ptr(3) report that at run time: they
describe the size mismatch and stop the program through ccol_fatal_err().
They find no mismatch, however, between two types of the same width, so
the types here are a promise that the caller keeps, not one that the
compiler checks. A key of the wrong width gets a report instead of an
acceptance: the raw entry points return ccol_invalid_args for it. The order
of the tree has a meaning only for keys of the width that the caller
created the map for.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void bump(cbmap scores, int who) {
    cbmap_redeclare(scores, int, int);
    int *slot = cbmap_get_ptr(scores, who);
    if (slot) {
        (*slot)++;
    }
}

cbmap_construct(scores, int, int);
int who = 7, score = 42;
cbmap_insert(scores, who, score);
bump(scores, 7);
cbmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_get (3),
.BR cbmap_get_ptr (3),
.BR cbmap_insert (3)
